"A 3.2-gigahertz processor goes through 3.2 billion cycles every second. Does that mean it carries out exactly 3.2 billion instructions every second? Why or why not?": "15ccab7d434f",
"A program is a list of instructions kept in memory. The processor has a small counter that holds the place of the next instruction. To fetch, the processor looks at the counter and copies the instruction at that place out of memory. In the model, the counter starts at 0, so the first instruction comes from cell 0.": "20f95c181162",
"After that, the counter goes up by one, and the three steps begin again with the next instruction. Adding 3 and 4 takes four instructions. The fourth kind of instruction, JUMP, is different. It changes the counter itself, so the processor goes back and repeats instructions it has already done. That is how a program only a few lines long can keep a processor busy all day. The fifth, STOP, ends the program.": "5ca7be1c2adb",
"Could you fetch my glasses from the kitchen?": "183148e19505",
"Each step is tiny, so the power of a processor comes from speed. A clock inside it sets the pace. Clock speed is measured in gigahertz, which means billions of cycles per second: a 3.2-gigahertz processor goes through 3.2 billion cycles every second. A cycle is not exactly one instruction. Some instructions take several cycles, and sometimes several instructions finish in one. Many processors also contain more than one core, so the work can be shared among them.": "753166630c20",
"In Lesson 5 you found the processor, a small square hiding under a big cooler, and you learned that it carries out the instructions of programs. But what does that actually look like? The answer is surprisingly plain. A processor does the same three small steps, over and over, for as long as the power is on. It fetches an instruction, decodes it, and executes it.": "c75b4ab7a2c1",
"In Lesson 5 you found the processor, a small square hiding under a big cooler, and you learned that it carries out the instructions of programs. But what does that actually look like? The answer is surprisingly plain. A processor does the same three small steps, over and over, for as long as the power is on. It fetches an instruction, decodes it, and executes it. A program is a list of instructions kept in memory. The processor has a small counter that holds the place of the next instruction. To fetch, the processor looks at the counter and copies the instruction at that place out of memory. In the model, the counter starts at 0, so the first instruction comes from cell 0. Next the processor decodes the instruction, which means working out what it is asking for. Our model understands only five instructions. LOAD copies a number from memory into the processor. ADD adds a number from memory to it, using an adder like the one in Lesson 4. STORE copies the result back into memory. Then the processor executes the instruction: it does that one small thing and nothing more. After that, the counter goes up by one, and the three steps begin again with the next instruction. Adding 3 and 4 takes four instructions. The fourth kind of instruction, JUMP, is different. It changes the counter itself, so the processor goes back and repeats instructions it has already done. That is how a program only a few lines long can keep a processor busy all day. The fifth, STOP, ends the program. Each step is tiny, so the power of a processor comes from speed. A clock inside it sets the pace. Clock speed is measured in gigahertz, which means billions of cycles per second: a 3.2-gigahertz processor goes through 3.2 billion cycles every second. A cycle is not exactly one instruction. Some instructions take several cycles, and sometimes several instructions finish in one. Many processors also contain more than one core, so the work can be shared among them. Keeping the program in the same memory as the numbers was one of the great ideas in computing. In 1945 the mathematician John von Neumann described a stored-program computer in a widely read report. On June 21, 1948, at the University of Manchester in England, a machine nicknamed the Baby ran the first program ever stored in the memory of an electronic computer. It was seventeen instructions long. With a stored program, the same processor, doing the same three steps, can become a calculator, a game, or a music player. You only have to load a different program.": "4b91f8e34d21",
